        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Paddle to Create Spin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A textured face lets the ball grip, but the spin comes from brushing across the ball. A flat swing with the grittiest paddle produces almost no rotation. Work on swing path and a loose grip alongside the new paddle, or you will be disappointed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Altering the Hitting Face</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sanding, coating or taping the face to add grip can make an approved paddle illegal for sanctioned play and can damage the carbon. Clean the face with a damp cloth and leave the texture as the manufacturer made it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Grit Wear</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Raw carbon texture wears down with play. If your slices start floating and your drives sail long, the face may be worn smooth. Check the face regularly and budget for replacement if spin is central to your game.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Elongated for the Wrong Game</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elongated paddles help topspin drives and serves but are slower in fast hand battles. If you mostly play doubles at the kitchen line, a hybrid or standard shape may give you more usable spin and fewer missed volleys.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking the Approved List</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Approval belongs to a specific model and generation. Buying an older, renamed or used paddle without checking the list can leave you with a paddle you cannot use in a sanctioned tournament.
              </p>
            </div>
