        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pickleball paddle for spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most players, the Six Zero Double Black Diamond Control 16mm is our pick. Six Zero lists a raw Toray 700K carbon face, and retailers list a 16mm core, a hybrid length of about 16.3 inches and an average weight around 8.1 ounces. That combination grips the ball well and stays quick at the net. Budget buyers should look at the Vatic Pro Flash 16mm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do raw carbon paddles really give more spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Raw carbon faces are textured and unpainted, so the ball grips rather than skids when you brush across it. That lets more of your swing's spin transfer to the ball than a smooth painted or fiberglass face. The paddle does not create spin on its own, though: your swing path and paddle speed still produce most of it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 14mm or 16mm better for spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many players find 16mm cores easier for spin on soft shots because they feel softer and hold the ball a fraction longer, which helps shape dinks and drops. Thinner 13 to 14mm cores add pop on drives. Choose based on whether your game leans toward control or power, then pick a textured carbon face.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does the grit on a spin paddle last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often you play, whether you play outdoors and how the texture was made. Players who play several times a week often report a noticeable loss of grip after a few months. Wiping the face after play and avoiding court scrapes slows the wear, but texture does not last forever.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are spin paddles legal in tournaments?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as the exact model is on USA Pickleball's approved list and the face has not been altered. USA Pickleball caps surface roughness, and reports in 2025 and 2026 describe a move toward a spin-rate test. Check the current equipment standards and the approved list before a sanctioned event.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I add grip to my paddle face for more spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Adding coatings, tape or abrasives to the hitting face can make the paddle illegal for sanctioned play and can damage the carbon. Clean the face with a damp microfiber cloth to remove dust and grime instead, and replace the paddle when the texture is worn out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a spin paddle good for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A raw carbon paddle is fine for beginners, and many budget options like the Vatic Pro Flash cost about the same as older fiberglass paddles. But beginners gain more from consistency and placement than from spin. A 16mm control paddle with a forgiving sweet spot is a sensible first choice either way.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
